# A click the rules will not take is refused here, not by the engine.
#
# The bug this pins, from a real 13x13 endgame (2026-08-16): the app had no
# legality check of its own on the way out. Every click left as
# `play <colour> <point>` and whatever GNU Go answered decided it, so a misclick
# onto a stone that had been sitting there for ten moves came back as
#
#   gnugo >> ? illegal move (command: play B M10)
#
# — an *error*, which the message-log sink takes and puts a red badge in front
# of the user. Two of them, in fact: the blank line that ends every GTP response
# was logged as a second, empty error. Those two lines were the entire contents
# of last_run.log for that session.
#
# So the assertion that matters here is `log_badge none`. Everything else about
# a refused click was already invisible — the record does not move either way —
# and asserting only move_count would pass with the engine still being asked and
# still complaining. The mock engine implements the rules, so it answers
# `? illegal move` exactly as GNU Go did.
#
# Both players are human, so nothing advances on its own and every click below
# is the user's.

new_game 9
wait_idle
switch_player black human
switch_player white human
wait_idle
expect log_badge none

# Black plays 4,4. Two clicks: one to take the stone out of the bowl, one to put
# it down.
click 4 4
click 4 4
wait_until move_count >= 1
wait_idle
expect move_count 1
expect black_stones 1
expect color_to_move W

# --- 1. An occupied point ----------------------------------------------------
# White reaches for a stone and puts it down on Black's.
click 2 2
wait_idle
expect holds_stone true

click 4 4
wait_idle
# Nothing recorded, nothing on the board, nobody asked.
expect move_count 1
expect white_stones 0
expect color_to_move W
expect log_badge none
# And the stone is still in hand, which is the whole of the feedback: it is what
# a real board does when you try to put a stone where one already is. It used to
# be dropped back into the bowl by the review path and held here, for no reason
# either way.
expect holds_stone true

# The same stone then goes down on a legal point, so the refusal cost the user
# nothing — one more click, not a new turn.
click 6 6
wait_until move_count >= 2
wait_idle
expect move_count 2
expect white_stones 1
expect holds_stone false
expect color_to_move B
expect log_badge none

# --- 2. Self-capture ---------------------------------------------------------
# The rule is the board's, not the engine's, so it covers more than occupancy.
# Black surrounds the 0,0 corner; White then tries to play into it.
click 0 1
click 0 1
wait_until move_count >= 3
wait_idle

click 8 0
click 8 0
wait_until move_count >= 4
wait_idle

click 1 0
click 1 0
wait_until move_count >= 5
wait_idle
expect move_count 5
expect black_stones 3

# White into the corner: no liberties, no capture. Refused.
click 0 0
wait_idle
expect holds_stone true
click 0 0
wait_idle
expect move_count 5
expect holds_stone true
expect color_to_move W
expect log_badge none

# --- 3. The refusal is a rules answer, not a policy one ----------------------
# availableActions() still says White may play — it is this point that is
# closed, not the turn. Keeping the two apart is why the check lives next to the
# board rather than in availableActions(), which knows nothing about points.
expect can_play true
expect phase playing

# And White can still play elsewhere.
click 4 0
wait_until move_count >= 6
wait_idle
expect move_count 6
expect white_stones 3       # 6,6 and 8,0 from before, plus this one
expect log_badge none
